systemd: fall back to a plain credentials directory without fsopen()

setup_credentials_internal() mounts a dedicated file system for a unit's
credentials and already carries a fallback to a plain directory, but reaches it
only for privilege errors.  fsmount_credentials_fs() starts with fsopen(), which
is Linux 5.2 and returns ENOSYS on this kernel; ENOSYS is not a privilege error,
so the fallback is skipped and the whole step fails.

Found while measuring what 0011 does not cover.  Stage-2 attempt 6 left 23 failed
units, and restarting them one at a time under SIGRTMIN+22 showed they do not
share a cause.  systemd-sysctl and systemd-journald die earlier than the
STATX_MNT_ID units, at step CREDENTIALS rather than NAMESPACE, and on a different
errno:

  systemd-sysctl.service: Failed to mount credentials fs: Function not implemented
  systemd-sysctl.service: Failed to set up credentials: Function not implemented
  systemd-sysctl.service: Failed at step CREDENTIALS spawning .../systemd-sysctl: Function not implemented
  systemd[1]: systemd-sysctl.service: Main process exited, code=exited, status=243/CREDENTIALS

The "statx() does not support 'STATX_ATTR_MOUNT_ROOT' attribute" line that
precedes it comes from the path_is_mount_point() at the top of the same function
and is not the failure: 0006's parent-comparison fallback answers it, dir_mounted
comes back false, and execution continues to the fsopen().

Units that configure no credentials never reach this and fail at NAMESPACE
instead, which is why the two bugs were visible only as one undifferentiated pile
of failures until each unit was restarted on its own.

Treating "the kernel cannot do this" like "we are not allowed to do this" is
sound here rather than merely convenient: both leave the caller with no mount,
and the fallback is upstream's own code for that situation.  It needs nothing
newer than 4.9 -- mkdir, open, rename, and a renameat2(RENAME_EXCHANGE) from 3.15
on the already-populated path.  What is given up is that the credentials live in
a plain directory under the runtime prefix, which is tmpfs here, instead of a
dedicated mount that can be reconfigured read-only.

Upstream is unlikely to take this: systemd's baseline is far above 4.9.

--- a/src/core/exec-credential.c
+++ b/src/core/exec-credential.c
@@ -1048,8 +1048,14 @@
         }
 
         mfd = fsmount_credentials_fs(&fs_fd);
-        if (ERRNO_IS_NEG_PRIVILEGE(mfd) && !dir_mounted) {
-                log_debug_errno(mfd, "Lacking privilege to mount credentials fs, falling back to plain directory.");
+
+        /* fsmount_credentials_fs() opens with fsopen(), and the rest of this function moves the result into
+         * place with move_mount(); both are Linux 5.2. Below that fsopen() fails with ENOSYS, which is not a
+         * privilege error, so this fallback was skipped and the whole step failed instead. A kernel that
+         * cannot provide the mount and a manager that is not allowed to create it leave the caller in the
+         * same position, so treat them the same and use the plain directory. */
+        if ((ERRNO_IS_NEG_PRIVILEGE(mfd) || ERRNO_IS_NEG_NOT_SUPPORTED(mfd)) && !dir_mounted) {
+                log_debug_errno(mfd, "Cannot mount credentials fs, falling back to plain directory.");
                 return setup_credentials_plain_dir(context, cred_dir);
         }
         if (mfd < 0)
